# Normalise line endings. The repo is authored on macOS (LF) and also worked on
# from Windows; without this, Git for Windows checks out CRLF and the next
# commit from that machine shows thousands of changed lines with no real diff.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two launchers, each pinned to its own platform. dev.cmd is read by
# cmd.exe, which wants CRLF; dev-mac.command is read by bash, which wants LF and
# would choke on the stray carriage returns. Left to "eol=lf" above, the Windows
# one was being checked out with Unix endings -- harmless for four simple lines,
# but not something to leave to luck in a file that may grow a label or a goto.
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.command text eol=lf

# Binary/asset types Git must never try to normalise.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp4  binary
*.mov  binary
